  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington is firing on all cylinders\u2014up 11% yesterday and week-to-date up 13%\u2014but the headline is productivity, not volume. Your five groomers hit $533 per groomer-day, 12% above the trailing 30-day baseline, which means the team is running genuinely hot. Retail jumped 65%, signaling strong front-of-house momentum. However, month-to-date Port is down 5.9% versus DOW-adjusted expectation, a real performance gap that Halloween this Saturday will deepen before it recovers. The lapse pool of 516 customers represents $318K in annual value\u2014that's your reactivation runway.\n\nHicksville is the opposite signal. Yesterday's grooming revenue fell 8% and add-on attach collapsed from 25% to 15%, which is an operational red flag on upsell discipline, not a capacity miss (you had four groomers). That said, month-to-date Hicksville is up 9.9% against DOW-adjusted expectation, so the week-long surge is real even if yesterday was soft. Productivity per groomer at $374 is 9% below baseline\u2014the team has room to tighten. Retail is strong (up 36%), which partially offsets the grooming dip. Zero bather hours is a structural gap you should own.\n\nGlen Cove is too small to drive the business, but the 88% add-on attach yesterday is exceptional; that's precision work. However, you've scheduled zero groomers for today and tomorrow\u2014confirm that's intentional. Port Washington and Hicksville combined are your core; watch Port's DOW-adjusted miss tighten through early November and Hicksville's upsell discipline return to baseline.\n\nPort Washington today: 7 groomers, 15 bather hours; Hicksville today: 4 groomers, zero bather hours; Glen Cove: zero groomer-days; next 7 days across both stores: 58 groomer-days total, 83 bather hours (concentrated at Port)."
